    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Christian democracy has drawn mainly from Catholic social teaching and neo-scholasticism, as well as the neo-Calvinist tradition, and later gained ground with Lutherans and Pentecostals.</li><li>Christian democrats are usually described as centre-left on economics and centre-right on many social and moral issues: they have often rejected liberal economics and individualism while defending private property against excessive state intervention.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-adenauer">Konrad Adenauer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-schuman">Robert Schuman</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcide-de-gasperi">Alcide De Gasperi</a>, three significant men at the beginning of the European project, were all Christian democrats.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The papal encyclical Graves de communi re prohibited Christian democracy as a political label, so Luigi Sturzo used the term popularism for the same idea in the political sphere.</p></aside>
    </section>

