    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1870s</span><span class="rf-text">Catholic political movements arise independently of the church to defend Catholic interests against liberal states</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1891</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiii">Pope Leo XIII</a> issues Rerum Novarum, which gives Catholic labour movements an intellectual platform</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1919</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s Christian democratic movements coalesce into the Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1931</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-xi">Pope Pius XI</a> issues <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quadragesimo-anno">Quadragesimo Anno</a> on the 40th anniversary of Rerum Novarum</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1933</span><span class="rf-text">The Centre Party and the Christian Social People's Service sign the Enabling Act after the Nazis take power, and both then dissolve</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1946</span><span class="rf-text">The Popular Republican Movement becomes the largest party in the French parliament</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1976</span><span class="rf-text">The European People's Party is founded, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> of Christian Democrats of 1965</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1990</span><span class="rf-text">Corneliu Coposu re-enrols his party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> as the Christian Democratic National Peasants' Party</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-revolution.jpg" alt="French Revolution" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonymous_-_Prise_de_la_Bastille.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unidentified painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The origins of Christian democracy go back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, when French republicanism and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> were deeply hostile to one another. The revolutionary governments attacked the church, confiscated its lands, persecuted its priests and attempted to establish new religions, first the Cult of Reason and then the Cult of the Supreme Being. In the decades that followed, the church saw the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a> as a threat to Catholic values, and it saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>, industrialisation and urbanisation as destroying traditional communal and family life. According to the Catholic Church, liberal economics promoted selfishness and materialism, and for much of the 19th century the church was hostile to democracy and liberalism.</p><p>That hostility was challenged by liberal Catholics, who believed the alliance between the church and aristocracy was a barrier to the church's mission. They wanted Catholics involved in parties, public action and parliamentarianism, although at first this was not an endorsement of democracy; Félicité de La Mennais, a leading figure, later became more accepting of it. Frédéric Ozanam, the founder of the Society of St Vincent de Paul, criticised economic liberalism and the commodification of labour and argued that labour associations and state intervention were needed; Luigi Sturzo credited him as the first Christian democrat. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, Wilhelm von Ketteler encouraged Catholics to accept the modern state and argued for productive associations with profit sharing, Christian trade unions and general workers' rights.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas.jpg" alt="Thomas Aquinas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St-thomas-aquinasFXD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carlo Crivelli, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>No single author has been recognised by all Christian democrats as the leading thinker, but Jacques Maritain comes closest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> and his thought on natural law, human dignity and the family were the philosophical roots, revived in the neo-scholasticism that followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiii">Pope Leo XIII</a>'s encyclical Aeterni Patris; the neo-Calvinist strand relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a>'s ideas of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of God and common grace. Academics have named several key ideas. Personalism, generally linked to Emmanuel Mounier, holds that humans are free beings with dignity and political rights, to be used for the common good, and it opposes both individualist and collectivist views of humanity. Pluralism has a vertical sense in subsidiarity, under which civil issues are first addressed at the lowest level of government, and a horizontal sense in sphere sovereignty, under which sectors such as family, education, economy and state each have autonomy and responsibility over their own sphere. The other ideas are solidarity, popularism and stewardship.</p><p>Christian democrats are usually socially conservative, generally sceptical of abortion and same-sex marriage, and they stress the individual's duties to a community. They also emphasise social justice, a welfare state, labour unions and regulation of market forces, and most European Christian democrats reject class struggle in favour of co-determination. Over time they moved from solidarism to the social market economy, which rests on a free price system and private property but adds a comprehensive social welfare system.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abraham-kuyper"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abraham-kuyper.jpg" alt="Abraham Kuyper" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abraham-kuyper">Abraham Kuyper</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abraham_Kuyper_1905_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the 1870s Catholic political movements arose independently of the church to defend Catholic interests against liberal states, especially over education. The parties that came from them included the Centre Party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the Catholic Party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, Catholic parties in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and the Christian Social Party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. At first they accepted the church's anti-liberal beliefs, but the effect of electoral politics pushed them to accept liberal democracy and to turn to voters rather than the church for legitimacy. Protestant confessional politics was wider and more varied. In the Netherlands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham-kuyper">Abraham Kuyper</a> founded the neo-Calvinist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-revolutionary-party">Anti-Revolutionary Party</a>; in Germany the Lutheran Adolf Stoecker established the Christian Social Party. Rerum Novarum in 1891 was the catalyst for the beginning of Christian democracy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and Austria.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, Christian democratic parties took shape in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, and in Germany Lutheran ordoliberals joined Catholics to form the Christian Democratic Union. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> the Christian Democrats were founded in 1964 amongst Pentecostals. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> early parties, formed in the early 20th century, were generally conservative and aimed to protect the interests of the Catholic church; after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-vatican-council">Second Vatican Council</a> of 1961 to 1963 Christian democrats became more progressive. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-the-berlin-wall">fall of the Berlin Wall</a>, Christian democratic parties made electoral gains in Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alcide-de-gasperi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alcide-de-gasperi.jpg" alt="Alcide De Gasperi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alcide-de-gasperi">Alcide De Gasperi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alcide_de_Gasperi_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Between the 1940s and 1990s Christian democratic parties were in power across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> for 36 years out of 50, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for 47 out of 52, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> for 47 out of 53 and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> for 49 out of 53. In Italy the Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> led the coalition government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcide-de-gasperi">Alcide De Gasperi</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the Popular Republican Movement became the largest party in parliament in 1946. They built corporatist welfare states, pressed for works councils and workers on boards, and encouraged regionalism in Germany, Italy and Belgium. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-adenauer">Konrad Adenauer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-schuman">Robert Schuman</a> and De Gasperi were significant at the beginning of the European project, and when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> Treaty was signed Christian democrats led the governments of four of the six countries.</p><p>The record in the interwar years was mixed. Corporatism, which the encyclical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quadragesimo-anno">Quadragesimo Anno</a> subjected to subsidiarity, inspired authoritarian and fascist regimes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, France, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, Portugal and Germany, and it fell out of political debate for that association. Sturzo's Italian People's Party was disbanded by the fascists. In Germany the Centre Party and the Christian Social People's Service signed the Enabling Act in 1933 and then dissolved; in Austria many Christian Socials helped build an authoritarian state. Stoecker, when his pro-worker policies failed to win the working class, turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antisemitism">anti-Semitism</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, COPEI helped establish democratic Venezuela and the Christian Democratic Party in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> was the main opposition to Pinochet.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The church itself resisted the early Catholic political movements, seeing them as a challenge to its control of the laity. In Graves de communi re the pope protested against the use of Christian democracy as a political label and preferred it as a description of a social movement. Some academics consider the Catholic parties of the early 20th century essentially Catholic rather than Christian democratic, while others count the Italian People's Party and the Popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as Christian democratic.</p><p>The Christian democratic stress on class mediation was challenged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberation-theology">liberation theology</a>, which stressed class conflict. Social democrats were hostile to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-communities">European Communities</a> until at least the mid-1980s; in the 1970s Swedish Prime Minister Olof Palme called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-commission">European Commission</a> conservative, capitalist, clerical and colonialist. Beginning in the 1980s European Christian democratic parties partly adopted neoliberal policies, and in Latin America the acceptance of neoliberal policies in the 1980s led to later electoral losses. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/2008-financial-crisis">2008 financial crisis</a> is said to have shown flaws in Christian democratic welfare. The Christian democratic status of some parties is also disputed: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>'s Justice and Development Party moved towards the tradition, but the link is questioned, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fidesz">Fidesz</a> was part of the European People's Party from 2004 to 2021, while its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viktor-orban">Viktor Orbán</a> claimed Hungary to be a Christian democracy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Christian democratic parties no longer have as much power in European politics. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> the Christian democratic party collapsed, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the Christian democrats were absorbed into Gaullist parties. The reasons for the decline are said to be several: European secularisation and the loss of a voting base, the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> and the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoliberalism">neoliberalism</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/2008-financial-crisis">2008 financial crisis</a>, and pressure from immigration and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/populism">populism</a>, which leaves the movement torn between the right's call for restrictions, business calls for an open labour market and the religious call for more charity to immigrants. Some parties, particularly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, no longer emphasise religion and have become more secular, while minor parties such as the Christian Union joined the European Christian Political Movement.</p><p>The Centrist Democrat International, formerly the Christian Democratic International, is the second-largest international political organisation in the world, after the Socialist International, and the European People's Party forms the largest group in the European Parliament. The ideas of subsidiarity and personalism are embedded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>. Many Muslim parties in Muslim countries have looked to the tradition for inspiration, among them parties in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> that have joined the Centrist Democrat International. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> there has been no major Christian democratic movement; Jacques Maritain saw America as the realisation of the ideal, and the American Solidarity Party is a minor party that identifies as Christian democratic.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Christian democracy is an ideology inspired by Christian social teaching, drawn mainly from Catholic social thought and neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a>. Christian democrats led postwar Western and Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in building welfare states and constructing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>, and their parties remain influential in Europe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>.</em></p>

